<!--
    angr "true_1804 usage if/else tail deduplication"
    (test_decompiling_true_1804_x86_64::usage) then the angr structurer ITE
    region-deduplication (the INVERSE of the SAILR duplication passes).

    kuna inherits Ghidra's collapse-based structurer, which, when a basic block is
    reachable from BOTH arms of an if/else, CLONES it into each arm instead of
    converging the two predecessors onto one copy.  On true_ubuntu1804::usage the
    trailing `if (v4 == 0) { A } else { B }` emits a shared online-help printf /
    setlocale / Full-documentation tail TWICE (loc 121 / 9 ifs vs angr's 112 / 6).
    angr emits each shared continuation once.  This is the exact inverse of
    gotoreduce/crossjumprevert/taildup (which DUPLICATE a shared tail to remove a
    goto): here kuna over-duplicated, and the readability win is to MERGE the copies.

    The `dedupitetail` pass (option-gated, default-off) does the sound, bounded
    subset of that convergence: when both arms of a 3-component if/else share a
    maximal common PREFIX or SUFFIX of *leaf* blocks that emit identical C (same
    printed-op sequence, constants matched by value, named storage by location, call
    targets by resolved callee), it hoists that shared run out of the if (prefix
    before it, suffix after) so it is emitted ONCE.  A prefix hoist is unconditionally
    sound (both arms run it first); a suffix hoist is sound when both arms' divergent
    middles fall through to it.

    usage's own duplication is sub-block-entangled (the online-help printf shares a
    basic block with the setlocale condition test), so it needs the FULL
    label-convergence transform and is out of scope for the bounded leaf hoist.  This
    testcase therefore uses a minimised x86-64 reproducer (gcc-faithful, exactly as
    the sibling who.o/morton SAILR tests do) that exhibits the same shared-tail shape
    at block granularity: an outer `if (x)` whose two arms each run a divergent guard
    (`if (cond_K()) body_K();`) and then call the SAME shared epilogue `shared(y)` in
    its own basic block, converging to `post(y)`.  That shared `shared(y)` block is
    reached from both arms - exactly the ISC-condensed shared continuation Ghidra's
    CollapseStructure (and kuna's region structurer) clones into each arm:

      g:    if (!x) goto else_arm;
            true_arm:  if (cond_a()) body_a();
            true_shared: shared(y); goto conv;       // shared SUFFIX block
            else_arm:  if (cond_b()) body_b();
            else_shared: shared(y); goto conv;        // DUP shared SUFFIX block
            conv:      post(y);
      cond_a/cond_b/body_a/body_b/shared/post: ret stubs.

    Pass 1 (option dedupitetail off, the DEFAULT): kuna clones the shared epilogue
    into each arm, so `shared(...)` appears in BOTH the `if` and `else` bodies (two
    clones).
    Pass 2 (option dedupitetail on): the shared suffix is hoisted out of the if (one
    copy after it), so `shared(...)` appears ONCE and a `dedupitetail:` warning is
    logged then assertion P2 (the merge log).

    The harness counts pattern occurrences across the WHOLE two-pass output, so the
    discriminating assertion is the TOTAL:
      * P1 pins the total `shared(` count at exactly THREE = two in pass 1 (the
        cloned epilogue, default-off) + one in pass 2 (the deduplicated copy).  With
        the merge log P2 proving the on-pass collapsed the duplication to a single
        copy, the only split consistent with a total of 3 is 2 (off) + 1 (on) -
        exactly the gap and its fix.
      * P3 pins the `post(` call (the convergence sink, NOT deduplicated by the pass)
        at exactly TWO = one per pass - a control that the pass touched only the
        shared suffix, not the rest of the function.
-->
